# `rates::hjm_paths`

Simulates the forward curve of the one-factor Heath-Jarrow-Morton model.

## Signature

```cpp
rates::HjmPaths rates::hjm_paths(std::int64_t steps, std::int64_t paths, double dt, const rates::HjmParams &params, const rates::SimOptions &options, pluss::Config cfg = {}, std::uint64_t seed = 0);
rates::HjmPaths rates::hjm_paths(std::int64_t steps, std::int64_t paths, double dt, const rates::HjmParams &params, pluss::Config cfg = {}, std::uint64_t seed = 0);
```

## What It Does

Follows the forward curve `f(t, τ)` on the tenor grid of `params` in the Musiela form: each step moves the curve along the tenor axis (the transport term), adds the no-arbitrage drift `σ(τ)∫₀^τ σ(u)du` and the shock `σ(τ)√dt·Z`, with one `Z` per path per step shared by all tenors. An extra node at time to maturity zero is the short rate. Because the transport is a forward difference stepped explicitly, `dt` may not exceed the smallest spacing of the tenor grid; the tenor grid also sets the accuracy (the interpolation smooths the curve by an amount of the order of half the spacing). The result is an [`HjmPaths`](HjmPaths.md). The two signatures differ in where the random-number settings come from.

## Parameters

`std::int64_t steps`
: Number of steps.

`std::int64_t paths`
: Number of paths.

`double dt`
: Step length in years; not larger than the smallest tenor spacing.

`const rates::HjmParams &params`
: The model.

`const rates::SimOptions &options`
: Random-number settings (first form).

`pluss::Config cfg`
: Device of the result.

`std::uint64_t seed`
: Seed (second form).

## Returns

An [`HjmPaths`](HjmPaths.md).

## Errors

- Invalid parameters or options, or `dt` above the smallest tenor spacing, throw (`pluss::Error`).

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

A year of weekly steps: the curve before and after, and the short rate.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace rt = fin::rates;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const rt::HjmParams p;
  const rt::HjmPaths sim = rt::hjm_paths(52, 4, 1.0 / 52.0, p, pluss::Config{}, 3);
  out.writeln("t = 0 :", sim.forward.select(0, 0).select(0, 0).items<double>());
  out.writeln("t = 1 :", sim.forward.select(0, 52).select(0, 0).items<double>());
  out.writeln("short rate at 0 and 1:", sim.rate.select(1, 0).select(0, 0).item<double>(), sim.rate.select(1, 0).select(0, 52).item<double>());
}
```

### Example 2

No-arbitrage: the average discount factor from the simulation reproduces the flat starting curve. Here the 2-year zero price from simulation against `e^{−r₀ T}`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <cmath>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace rt = fin::rates;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  const rt::HjmParams p;
  rt::SimOptions options;
  options.seed = 3;
  const double dt = 1.0 / 252.0;
  const rt::HjmPaths sim = rt::hjm_paths(504, 100000, dt, p, options);
  const Series integral = sim.rate.slice(0, 0, 504, 1).sum(0, false) * dt;
  out.writeln("simulated", (integral * -1.0).exp().mean().item<double>(), ", exact", std::exp(-0.03 * 2.0));
}
```

### Example 3

The step restriction: a step larger than the smallest tenor spacing (0.25 here) is refused, as the transport would be unstable.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <string>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace rt = fin::rates;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const rt::HjmParams p;
  try {
    (void)(rt::hjm_paths(4, 4, 0.5, p));
  } catch (const std::exception &e) {
    const std::string text(e.what());
    out.writeln(text.substr(0, text.find('\n')));
  }
}
```

### Example 4

Paths first, and one factor: every tenor on a path is moved by the same shock, so the forwards of one path move together.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace rt = fin::rates;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(0));
  rt::SimOptions options;
  options.paths_first = true;
  options.seed = 3;
  const rt::HjmPaths sim = rt::hjm_paths(26, 6, 1.0 / 52.0, rt::HjmParams{}, options);
  out.writeln("forward shape", sim.forward.sizes(), ", rate shape", sim.rate.sizes());
}
```

### Example 5

The curve is conserved on average. With a constant volatility the mean forward after a year stays near its starting 3% apart from the no-arbitrage drift, which is small (order `σ²τ`); the spread follows the volatility.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace rt = fin::rates;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  rt::HjmParams p;
  p.tenors = {0.25, 1.0, 3.0};
  p.vol = {0.010, 0.010, 0.010};
  const rt::HjmPaths sim = rt::hjm_paths(52, 50000, 1.0 / 52.0, p, pluss::Config{}, 3);
  const Series curve = sim.forward.select(0, 52);
  out.writeln("mean forward by tenor:", curve.mean(0, false).items<double>());
  out.writeln("sd of the forward by tenor:", curve.std(0, false).items<double>());
}
```

